hypotheses.statement:
For a given $\ell$, at most one index $j$ in the passage satisfies $\beta_\ell(s_j)>0$. If such $j$ exists, then, for every $i\in I$ and every integer $m\ge1$, $$\label{eq:prediction-single-hit}
 \beta_\ell\bigl(T_m(s_i,z_i)\bigr)=
 \begin{cases}
  \beta_\ell(s_j),&m=j-i,\\
  0,&m\ne j-i.
 \end{cases}$$ Here $\beta_\ell$ is the function on $\mathbb R$ used in <a href="#eq:input" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:input">[eq:input]</a>, not its periodization.

Let $j$ be an index in the fixed complete passage and let $\ell$ satisfy $\beta_\ell(s_j)>0$. Then $|v_{j,\ell}|\le1/2$, $S(v_{j,\ell})=v_{j,\ell}$, and $$\label{eq:register-at-readout}
 v_{j,\ell}=\frac{\beta_\ell(s_j)}{10}
 \sum_{i\in I}\alpha(s_i)\chi(z_i)
 \left(\frac{z_i}{|z_i|}\right)^{2^{j-i+1}}.$$ This conclusion does not assume any alignment of the summands’ phases.

In a complete passage with finite main coordinate, let $i_0=\min I$. For every $i_0\le n<N_{k+1}$, $z_n\ne0$ and $$\label{eq:square-lower-bound}
 |z_{n+1}|\ge |z_n|^2,
 \qquad
 \frac{z_{n+1}}{|z_{n+1}|}
   =\left(\frac{z_n}{|z_n|}\right)^2.$$ Moreover, some index $N$ with $i_0\le N<N_{k+1}$ satisfies $|z_N|>1$.

